#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158295} :activity/id #uuid "0c713b1b-8699-5cdf-acd5-178d556abfff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495 :topic/id #uuid "a4841cae-d95f-5e07-960c-2030a42a2568" :topic/math-academy-id 2975} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158564 :tutorial/content "Recall that $f(x)$ is an **even function** if\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe graph of an even function is always symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/fc/fc57d05c6bef23243df97979c0680054c6ec66b836a97871970ee055c60eab4a.png)\n\nAlso recall that $g(x)$ is an **odd function** if\n\n$$\ng(-x) = -g(x).\n$$\n\nThe graph of an odd function always has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. In other words, if we rotate the graph $180^\\circ$ about the origin, the resulting graph looks the same.\n\n![](images/d2/d21ae289b641f26e3b43dc7035e2ea02147d44dc490f0ab5e6e34381ea4c2db3.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e9042a5a-4bbc-5b00-aec4-0982199b1ab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6806 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "33e51af5-883a-5d53-a82e-9983b0aab23d" :step/math-academy-id 41319 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157396 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156997 :question/id #uuid "9cfff4db-e33e-5a3c-a1d4-99e910f2c2fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-10897"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394813}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"} :step/id #uuid "9dffa825-7698-5068-9cf9-029959145880" :step/math-academy-id 41320 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158565 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the odd function $f(x)$ shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c179ee67a04113130e65e3ce4b8581c969724c380fbe903423d023427d573f8.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nbecause the positive and negative areas cancel each other out.\n\nFurthermore, by the adjacent intervals rule, we can write\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^0 f(x)  \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_{0}^a f(x)  \\, \\textrm dx = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^0 f(x)  \\, \\textrm dx = - \\int_{0}^a f(x)  \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9707c582-25f4-5558-b5b5-e6af4f83c071" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6807 :tutorial/title "Integrals of Odd Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a69a25f0-35d2-5e6f-99dc-f9e17ed11afa" :step/math-academy-id 41323 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156998 :question/id #uuid "a3e0d847-4dd1-5a47-948c-06959065b73f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10898"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382587}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"} :step/id #uuid "3dd1c1c0-5948-50f9-802e-942f886fd7e6" :step/math-academy-id 41324 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157398 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156999 :question/id #uuid "29ca6f83-ceb9-54fa-b8c4-6c0fbbd4b742" :question/math-academy-id "e-10911"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381139}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"} :step/id #uuid "3288d02d-a5b0-5d8a-8137-f96ae6e90b23" :step/math-academy-id 41371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158566 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our even function $f(x)$ once more.\n\n![](images/7a/7a1b83234d7d028484eb939146db822a087ebffa063ff74dcd3b73478acd0399.png)\n\nWhen we have a definite integral of an even function, we can often use symmetry to simplify it. \n\nFrom the picture above, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = A.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =A+A \\\\  & =2A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, if $f(x)$ is even, then\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx  = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nThis rule is helpful because definite integrals where one of the integration limits is zero are often easier to evaluate." :tutorial/id #uuid "340dd64d-312a-5516-b425-9cf0b512e446" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6850 :tutorial/title "Integrals of Even Functions"} :step/id #uuid "2b925bf3-52e2-5fd3-a231-2bd9005b1e1c" :step/math-academy-id 41640 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158567 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, it's worth remembering that the basic trigonometric functions can be classified as even or odd. \n\n- $\\cos x$ is an even function:\n\n$$\n\\cos(-x) = \\cos x\n$$\n\nThis function is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/68/684c18a8cbd2aa9cf478ff5a569add34a7157eea2fed7075a56a2cee2aec985f.png)\n\n- $\\sin x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\sin(-x) = -\\sin x\n$$\n\nThis function has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n![](images/91/911c92dd5f3d6924226b49f2f25aeba67c8dca676612ccf2830e3a15871a49d6.png)\n\n- $\\tan x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan x\n$$\n\nThis function has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n![](images/92/92e670859be89f70e0c8199a7b368e187ab30fd3682478832690ee842595b62f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6163a166-7434-5d31-846e-676e81407a7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6851 :tutorial/title "Trigonometric Functions"} :step/id #uuid "55d52e16-03f0-5165-836c-65302f301bf0" :step/math-academy-id 41641 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158296}}] :activity/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals Using Symmetry" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157523}]}
